//! Foundational values and contracts for writing Foster programs.
//!
//! `core` provides the building blocks used throughout the library: numbers, text,
//! lists, optional values, recoverable errors, and contracts for copying, cleanup,
//! and comparison. Start here when choosing how to represent a value. The
//! [standard library](std.html#module-overview) adds collections, algorithms, and
//! services such as files, networking, clocks, and randomness.
//!
//! ## Find a module
//!
//! | Need | Modules |
//! | --- | --- |
//! | Numbers and Boolean values | [Integers](core-int.html#module-overview), [floats](core-float.html#module-overview), [booleans](core-bool.html#module-overview) |
//! | Text and identifiers | [Strings and text cursors](core-string.html#module-overview), [Unicode scalars](core-code_point.html#module-overview), [symbols](core-symbol.html#module-overview) |
//! | Binary data | [A single byte](core-byte.html#module-overview), [immutable bytes](core-bytes.html#module-overview), [mutable byte buffers](core-bytes-buffer.html#module-overview) |
//! | Ordered values and ranges | [Lists](core-list.html#module-overview), [ranges](core-range.html#module-overview) |
//! | Absence and failure | [Option](core-option.html#module-overview), [Result](core-result.html#module-overview), [remote errors](core-remote_error.html#module-overview) |
//! | Shared behavioral contracts | [Copying](core-copy.html#module-overview), [cleanup](core-drop.html#module-overview), [comparison and hashing](core-ordering.html#module-overview), [callable aliases](core-functions.html#module-overview) |
//!
//! ## Using core
//!
//! Import the module whose operations you need, such as `import core.string`.
//! Importing `core` does not import every child module. Some foundational types
//! are always available to the language; explicit imports expose their full library APIs.
//!
//! `Option<T>` represents a value that may be absent. `Result<T, E>` represents
//! either a successful value or an error explaining failure. Their individual
//! module pages show how to inspect and transform those outcomes.
//!
//! String positions normally count grapheme clusters: the pieces of text a reader
//! sees as characters. A cluster can contain several Unicode scalars and UTF-8 bytes.
//! Choose explicit scalar or byte APIs when those are the units your work needs.
//!
//! Read each operation's ownership and boundary rules. For example, copying an
//! element out of a list can fail if the element does not support `Copy`, and an
//! integer operation can fail if its result does not fit in 64 bits. A familiar
//! method name does not imply automatic copying, clamping, or error recovery.
